      <p>Renting a home is a considered decision. Renters search by neighbourhood, size, budget and features, compare several communities, and come back to the same few websites more than once before they book a tour. Google Ads works well here when it follows that journey, and wastes money when it treats every click the same.</p>

      <h2>Build campaigns the way renters search</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Area searches:</strong> "apartments for rent in" a city or neighbourhood, the largest volume</li>
        <li><strong>Floor plan searches:</strong> studio, one bedroom, two bedroom, each with its own price point and availability</li>
        <li><strong>Feature searches:</strong> pet friendly, in-unit laundry, parking, furnished, luxury</li>
        <li><strong>Your community's name:</strong> brand searches, which listing sites and competitors may also be bidding on</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each group gets ads and landing pages that answer the search directly: the right floor plans, current availability, pricing and a clear way to book a tour.</p>

      <h2>Returning visitors are the best audience</h2>
      <p>Most renters do not book on their first visit. Remarketing lists for search ads (RLSA) let the account recognise people who have already been on your website when they search again, and bid more or show a tour offer to them.</p>
      <p>That is usually where cost per tour falls fastest, because these searchers already know the community and are deciding. The case study below came from exactly this approach.</p>

      <h2>Location targeting works differently for rentals</h2>
      <p>For most local businesses, Google's default location option, which includes people interested in your area as well as people in it, wastes money. Rentals are one of the exceptions. People moving for work or study search from another city before they arrive.</p>
      <p>Target the area and the places renters tend to move from, then check where tours actually come from. <a href="/learn/google-ads-location-targeting-settings/">Google Ads location targeting</a> explains both options.</p>

      <h2>Housing ads have special rules</h2>
      <p>In the United States and Canada, Google restricts personalised targeting for housing ads: you cannot target or exclude people based on gender, age, parental status, marital status or postcode. Ad copy and negative keywords must also respect fair housing law. Some exclusions that look like ordinary housekeeping can be discriminatory, so they need care.</p>
      <p>These rules shape the whole account structure, which is why campaigns are built around search terms, areas and floor plans rather than around audiences.</p>

      <h2>Budgets that follow vacancy</h2>
      <p>A fully leased building does not need ads. A new block of units coming available needs them urgently. Budgets should move with occupancy:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Separate campaigns per property in a portfolio, so spend can follow each one's availability</li>
        <li>Floor plans paused or reduced when they are fully leased</li>
        <li>More budget during the busiest leasing months in your market</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Measure tours and leases</h2>
      <p>Clicks and page views are easy to count and say little about leasing. The conversions that matter are tours booked, applications and signed leases.</p>
      <p>We track tour bookings and calls from the website, and where possible feed back which ones became leases from your property management software. Then the account learns which searches fill units, not just which ones fill the website. The <a href="/tools/utm-builder/">UTM builder</a> helps keep tracking consistent across listing sites and other channels.</p>

